# Topic Modeling vs. Human Coders: #WhyIStayed / #WhyILeft

Code for the structural topic model (STM) analysis in:

Rodriguez, M. Y., & Storer, H. (2020). A computational social science perspective on qualitative data exploration: 
Using topic models for the descriptive analysis of social media data*. Journal of Technology in Human Services, 38(1), 54–86. 
\url{https://doi.org/10.1080/15228835.2019.1616350}



The study compares structural topic models fit at different numbers of topics
(K) against manual/qualitative coding of tweets tagged `#WhyIStayed` and
`#WhyILeft`.

## Repository contents

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `STM_github_cleaned.R` | Full analysis script: co-occurrence heatmaps, text preprocessing, STM fitting at multiple K, model diagnostics, topic effect estimates, topic correlations, and word cloud/quote visualizations. |
| `JTHS_R&R2.5.19.RData` | Saved R workspace from the analysis session (fitted models, processed data, and intermediate objects), included so figures/results can be reproduced without re-running the full STM fitting process. |

## Requirements

- R (last tested on 4.5.3)
- R packages:
  ```r
  install.packages(c(
    "dplyr", "tm", "ggplot2", "caret", "gplots", "corrplot",
    "RColorBrewer", "stm", "streamR", "tidyverse", "tidytext",
    "stminsights", "stringr", "Rtsne", "rsvd", "geometry", "igraph"
  ))
  ```

## Data

The script expects three input files in a local `data/` folder (not included
in this repository — see note below):

- `data/forSTMfinal.csv` — tweet-level dataset (text + metadata) used to fit the STM models
- `data/whyileft_cooccurence.csv` — qualitative code co-occurrence matrix for `#WhyILeft`
- `data/whyistayed_cooccurence.csv` — qualitative code co-occurrence matrix for `#WhyIStayed`

 **Note:** The underlying tweet data is not published here. 

Figures and exported tables are written to a local `output/` folder.

## Usage

Two ways to work with this repo:

1. **Reproduce figures/results without refitting models (fast):** load
   `JTHS_R&R2.5.19.RData` into an R session, then run the plotting/inspection
   sections of `STM_github_cleaned.R` directly (the STM fits, `meta`,
   `vocab`, etc. are already in the workspace).
2. **Run the full analysis from scratch (slow — STM fitting and `searchK`
   diagnostics are computationally expensive):** place the three data files
   above in `data/`, then run `STM_github_cleaned.R` top to bottom.

The script is organized into sections matching the analysis workflow:
Visualize (code co-occurrence) → Ingest → Prepare → Estimate (fit STM models
at K = 97, auto-selected K, and K = 65) → Evaluate (`searchK` diagnostics) →
Understand (topic labels, summary plots, effect estimates, topic
correlations, word clouds/quotes) → interactive exploration via
[`stminsights`](https://cran.r-project.org/package=stminsights).

## Citation

If you use this code, please cite the paper above.
